    <tr>
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify main ideas and supporting details in spoken texts. Interactive: Ensure mutual understanding in group interactions through clarification and confirmation.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify key elements in spoken legends (e.g., "Who is the hero in the story?"). Can clarify and respond to questions about the order of events to support group interactions (e.g., "What happened first?"). Can summarize a story's events during discussions (e.g., "The hero saved the village").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of famous Panamanian legends (e.g., La Tulivieja, El Chivo, El Hombre Caimán) or general mythical creatures/heroes. Students briefly describe what they see. The teacher asks: "What do you know about legends?" "Do you have a favorite legend?" "What kind of characters are usually in a legend?" (e.g., heroes, monsters). This activates prior knowledge.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 core vocabulary words relevant to legends using flashcards or images: <i>legend, monster, hero, village, story, fight, save, brave, ancient, witch</i>. The teacher models the pronunciation of each word, emphasizing clear enunciation. The teacher also explicitly models the pronunciation of past tense -ed endings (e.g., "saved," "fought" — irregular verb, "celebrated") and -ing endings (e.g., "fighting"), highlighting the /t/, /d/, or /ɪd/ sounds for -ed. For example, by saying "The hero saved the village," pointing to "saved."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a <i>legend</i> a true story or an old, traditional story?" (Expected answer: An old story.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a <i>hero</i> usually good or evil?" (Expected answer: Good.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does a <i>monster</i> typically <i>save</i> or <i>fight</i> people?" (Expected answer: Fight.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Legends of Panama")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio narrative excerpt (1-2 minutes) of a simplified Panamanian legend (e.g., a version of "El Chivo"). The legend explicitly uses target vocabulary (<i>legend, hero, monster, village, story, fight, save, brave, ancient, witch</i>) and grammar (Past Simple to narrate events, Past Continuous to describe background actions, adverbs of time like "long ago," "suddenly"). Example narrative: "Long ago, in an ancient village, a brave hero was living peacefully. Suddenly, a terrible monster appeared, attacking the town. The hero bravely decided to fight the evil creature and save his people. He was fighting the monster all night. In the end, he rescued the village."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret spoken texts related to daily life and academic situations, identifying main ideas and specific details.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (First Listen):</b> The teacher plays the audio. Students listen and answer: "What is this story about? Is it a funny story, a sad story, or a legend about a hero?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listen):</b> The teacher provides 3-4 simple True/False statements related to the main ideas and some supporting details. Students listen again and mark T or F.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "The story happened last week." (F)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"A brave hero lived in the village." (T)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The monster saved the village." (F)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific details (main ideas, characters, events), using Past Simple and Past Continuous, practicing core vocabulary (<i>legend, monster, hero, village, story, fight, save, brave, ancient, witch</i>), and pronunciation of -ed endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Listening Gap-fill):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with the legend's transcript, but with key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>hero, monster, village, fight, save, brave</i>) and some past simple/continuous verbs missing. Students listen to the audio again (perhaps twice, pausing if necessary) and fill in the blanks. The teacher then reviews answers as a class, focusing on pronunciation of the filled-in words, especially -ed endings. Example: "The brave ______ (hero) was ______ (living) peacefully."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled (Listen and Sequence):</b> The teacher provides sentences from the legend (e.g., "The hero was living peacefully," "A terrible monster appeared," "The hero fought the creature," "He saved the village") written on separate strips of paper or displayed on the board in jumbled order. Students listen to the audio one last time and arrange the sentences in the correct chronological order. This reinforces sequencing events pragmatically and uses adverbs of time.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Listen and Clarify):</b> In pairs, students receive a short set of questions about the legend (e.g., "Who was the hero?", "What was the monster doing?", "What happened first?", "What happened after the monster appeared?"). Students discuss their answers, referring to their completed gap-fill and sequence. They practice clarifying details using phrases like, "What happened first?", "Did the hero fight or run away?", and confirming understanding. The teacher monitors, listening for correct use of vocabulary and past tenses.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Legends of Panama")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion and Short Presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces brief audio snippets (20-30 seconds each) of two *different* simplified Panamanian legends, briefly describing the main hero and antagonist, and one key event. Each snippet is played once or twice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a task sheet: "Which legend do you find most interesting? Discuss why, identifying the hero, the monster/antagonist, and one main action. Use the words: <i>legend, hero, monster, village, fight, save, brave</i>. Clarify with your group: 'What happened first in the story?' 'Did the brave hero save the village?'"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups listen carefully to the snippets, discuss their favorite, and prepare a short oral summary (2-3 sentences) about their chosen legend, incorporating the target vocabulary and sequencing phrases like "First,..." "Then,..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group briefly presents their "Favorite Legend" to the class, stating its name (if known, or a descriptive title), the hero, the monster, and the key event (e.g., "Our favorite legend is about 'El Chivo'. The brave hero was exploring the mountain when he encountered a scary monster. The hero fought the creature to save his town.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions and oral presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in group discussions to identify elements of spoken legends and clarify details, followed by a short oral presentation by each group summarizing their favorite legend using target vocabulary and sequencing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (especially words with -ed, -ing endings).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 3-5 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>hero, monster, fight, save, village</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify main ideas (hero, antagonist, key action) from spoken text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to clarify details with peers (e.g., "What happened first?", "Can you repeat that?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempt to sequence events in the summary using simple time markers.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about legends did you learn today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most important event in the legend we heard today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me when listening to the legends today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult, and how can I improve my listening comprehension for stories?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in identifying main ideas and supporting details in spoken texts?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What vocabulary words or grammatical structures (Past Simple/Continuous) need more reinforcement?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the clarification and sequencing activities effective for interactive listening? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (e.g., more visual support, slower audio, explicit sentence frames for clarification)?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In the next lesson, we will *read* a short Panamanian legend that uses many of these same vocabulary words and past tense events. This will help us further understand the context and reinforce our knowledge of legends."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">___</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>